1
00:00:01,330 --> 00:02:53,294
(Music)

2
00:02:53,294 --> 00:02:58,758
(Music ends)

3
00:02:58,758 --> 00:03:10,478
(Applause and cheers)

4
00:03:10,478 --> 00:03:13,522
Thank you so much and hello everyone.

5
00:03:13,522 --> 00:03:17,777
I am 18 years old, and I am from Eugene, Oregon.

6
00:03:17,777 --> 00:03:21,113
I've been playing the accordion for about 12 years,

7
00:03:21,113 --> 00:03:23,199
and it is such a pleasure to be here

8
00:03:23,199 --> 00:03:26,661
and to share the beauty and versatility of my instrument

9
00:03:26,661 --> 00:03:28,412
with all of you.

10
00:03:28,412 --> 00:03:31,249
Recently, I had the privilege of performing

11
00:03:31,249 --> 00:03:34,502
for NPR's Tiny Desk Concerts.

12
00:03:34,502 --> 00:03:35,670
Thank you.

13
00:03:35,670 --> 00:03:38,923
(Applause)

14
00:03:38,923 --> 00:03:42,885
And there, I performed the piece that I have just played for you,

15
00:03:42,885 --> 00:03:45,554
called "The Prelude and Fugue"

16
00:03:45,554 --> 00:03:48,474
by one of my favorite classical composers,

17
00:03:48,474 --> 00:03:51,018
Johann Sebastian Bach.

18
00:03:51,018 --> 00:03:54,522
Now I love playing music in many different styles,

19
00:03:54,522 --> 00:03:58,317
from Bach to [The] Beatles to AC/DC --

20
00:03:58,317 --> 00:04:02,154
especially in a duet with my father.

21
00:04:02,154 --> 00:04:03,864
He is my teacher

22
00:04:03,864 --> 00:04:07,660
and a professional accordion player himself.

23
00:04:07,660 --> 00:04:10,496
Now before I play my final piece for you,

24
00:04:10,496 --> 00:04:14,125
I'd like to briefly familiarize you with this instrument.

25
00:04:14,125 --> 00:04:17,795
So this is a professional model of a B-system,

26
00:04:17,795 --> 00:04:20,047
chromatic button accordion,

27
00:04:20,047 --> 00:04:23,426
which has 120 buttons on the left side

28
00:04:23,426 --> 00:04:26,220
and only 106 on the right side.

29
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:27,430
Not that many.

30
00:04:27,430 --> 00:04:30,099
There are also registers over here,

31
00:04:30,099 --> 00:04:33,185
which I use to change the tone of the sound,

32
00:04:33,185 --> 00:04:38,524
so I can imitate other instruments such as the bassoon, the piccolo,

33
00:04:38,524 --> 00:04:41,027
the organ and so on.

34
00:04:41,027 --> 00:04:43,904
And they are also duplicated up here.

35
00:04:43,904 --> 00:04:46,157
And these are called chin switches,

36
00:04:46,157 --> 00:04:47,533
which, as you can guess,

37
00:04:47,533 --> 00:04:51,829
I can operate using my chin, like this.

38
00:04:51,829 --> 00:04:56,542
And additionally, this middle part here, which I constantly push and pull,

39
00:04:56,542 --> 00:04:58,502
is called the bellows.

40
00:04:58,502 --> 00:05:02,173
And the bellows act as the lungs of the accordion

41
00:05:02,173 --> 00:05:07,094
since they pump air in and out and control the dynamics.

42
00:05:07,094 --> 00:05:11,140
Now considering this instrument weighs about 35 pounds,

43
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:12,933
I would say it's a bit of a workout,

44
00:05:12,933 --> 00:05:16,937
as well as an intense brain exercise to handle.

45
00:05:16,937 --> 00:05:18,564
And you may be wondering

46
00:05:18,564 --> 00:05:21,942
why I chose to play such a unique instrument.

47
00:05:21,942 --> 00:05:23,110
Well when I was little,

48
00:05:23,110 --> 00:05:26,989
we actually had this tiny little accordion in our living room,

49
00:05:26,989 --> 00:05:30,284
and one day I just came up to my dad and said,

50
00:05:30,284 --> 00:05:32,995
"Hey, can you teach me how to play this?"

51
00:05:32,995 --> 00:05:37,375
So I started off by learning a few chords and simple melodies,

52
00:05:37,375 --> 00:05:40,252
and I just fell in love with the instrument,

53
00:05:40,252 --> 00:05:43,589
even though the first-ever accordion I played

54
00:05:43,589 --> 00:05:45,966
didn't even have all the notes.

55
00:05:45,966 --> 00:05:51,180
So I would say that having a teacher at home living with me

56
00:05:51,180 --> 00:05:54,225
also helped me progress pretty quickly,

57
00:05:54,225 --> 00:05:57,645
which has brought me to where I am today.

58
00:05:57,645 --> 00:05:59,855
And now I would like to play for you

59
00:05:59,855 --> 00:06:06,570
"Moments Musicaux" by composer Franz Schubert.

60
00:06:06,570 --> 00:06:14,245
(Applause)

61
00:06:14,245 --> 00:07:50,341
(Music)

62
00:07:50,341 --> 00:07:55,638
(Music ends)

63
00:07:55,638 --> 00:07:59,638
(Applause and cheers)
